@ 37.1.2 - Proto-Iroquoian to Proto-Northern Iroquoian
# citation: cedh audmanh, from Julian, Charles (2010), “A History of the Iroquoian Languages”,
# 			University of Manitoba, Winnipeg
	{ o, o:[+ long] } { u, u:[+ long] } > { a, a:[+ long] } { o, o:[+ long] }
	ij{ i, i:[+ long] } > h{ i, i:[+ long] } / {k,s}_ “(possibly after all non-glottal obstruents)”
	i > e / _:[- long] // _hCC
	∅ > { i, i:[+ long] } / #_(C)(C)CVC(C)(C)#
	V > ”V / “in antepenultimate syllables, if the vowel of the penultimate syllable was short
				*/a/ followed by a single non-glottal consonant”
	V > ”V / “in penultimate syllables not preceded by an accented antepenult”
	V:[+ long] > V:[- long] // in U#
	”V > ”V:[+ long] / “in open penultimate syllables followed by a non-glottal consonant”
	h > ∅ / #_s
	n > ∅ / _ti
	t > ∅ / n_V